Hecks.hecksagon "Tools" do
  uses_framework "Governance"
  # ============================================================
  # TOOLS — adapter bindings : <Category>Tool.X dispatch → real run
  # ============================================================
  #
  # The Tools bluebook (sibling file tools.bluebook) declares five
  # category aggregates as first-class dispatches : ShellTool, FileTool,
  # SearchTool, WebTool, Cascade. The default-to-memory runtime is
  # enough for the dispatch + event-emit loop ; this hecksagon adds the
  # SIDE-EFFECT layer.
  #
  # Each `adapter :claude_tool` / `adapter :web_tool` entry below binds
  # one command to the corresponding tool kind. When the runtime sees a
  # matching dispatch, the adapter fires, the underlying tool actually
  # runs (real shell, real file edit, real read), and the result routes
  # back into the Cascade aggregate as a follow-on dispatch.
  #
  # ── The principle the binding sits on ──
  #
  #   "the domain will compose the tool call and the adapter runs
  #    them" — Chris, 2026-05-12
  #
  # The bluebook (composition) is pure ; the adapter (execution) is
  # the only impure layer. Same shape as the IO monad — intent and
  # side-effect kept separate.
  #
  # ── Cascade target ──
  #
  # `result_into: "HecksPlayground::Framework::Cascade::Cascade.RecordResult"` — after the kernel hook runs,
  # the captured outcome cascades into a Cascade record, joinable
  # against the originating invocation by id. Previously the cascade
  # target was the same Tools aggregate ; the category split moves the
  # outcome chain to its own aggregate, separating "the act" from
  # "what came back".


  # ── THE EXECUTION PORT (2026-08-12) ──
  #
  # These three binds replace seven `adapter :claude_tool, command: "...",
  # tool: :bash, result_into: "..."` lines that had been here since the Rust
  # runtime, and that DID NOTHING once hecksagain was vendored in.
  # HecksagonBuilder#adapter parses that form into `@raw_adapters`, and
  # #build never passes @raw_adapters into IR::Hecksagon — every one of them
  # was discarded at boot. The door dispatched, recorded, emitted BashRan,
  # and executed nothing ; a `Tools::ShellTool.Bash` writing a probe file
  # reported ✓ and the file did not exist. Removed rather than left inert:
  # a declaration that reads as wiring and isn't is worse than no
  # declaration, because it makes the missing edge invisible.
  #
  # The replacement says the same thing in hecksagain's own idiom — an
  # `execution` port (verb `executed_by`, signal :reply, declared in this
  # corpus at aggregates/framework/ports/execution.port -- NOT present in
  # the published hecks gem 1.3.0) with one adapter
  # per tool category. These are ordinary binds, so Registry::Verification
  # type-checks them at boot exactly as it does `persisted_by` : an adapter
  # that does not implement `executed_by` now fails LOUDLY at wiring instead
  # of silently doing nothing. The principle this file already stated —
  # "the domain will compose the tool call and the adapter runs them" — is
  # unchanged ; only the mechanism carrying it is real now.
  #
  # The outcome still chains into Cascade.RecordResult, but the dispatcher
  # does it off the port's reply rather than each binding naming its own
  # `result_into` — one place, and no stale FQN to drift (the old strings
  # named "HecksPlayground::Framework::Cascade::Cascade", and one of them
  # "Cascade::Cascade::Cascade" ; the real verb is Cascade::Cascade).
  Tools::ShellTool.executed_by("Shell")
  Tools::FileTool.executed_by("Filesystem")
  Tools::SearchTool.executed_by("Search")

  # ── WebTool : :web_tool adapter family (i569) ──
  #
  # Bind WebTool.WebFetch / WebTool.WebSearch to the :web_tool adapter
  # family declared at framework/adapter_families/web_tool.hecksagon.
  # Same shape as the :claude_tool bindings above : `command` names
  # the dispatch target, `tool` selects the family's behavior
  # (web_fetch → perform_web_fetch hook ; web_search →
  # perform_web_search hook), and `result_into` chains the kernel-
  # hook output back into Cascade.RecordResult.
  #
  # Runtime status : i594 has landed — the runtime auto-dispatches these
  # :web_tool bindings after a matching command via
  # `Runtime::resolve_web_tool_adapters`, chaining into Cascade.RecordResult.

  # ── EmailTool : execution port (i757) ──
  #
  # REPLACES the old :mcp / :web_tool "adapter" declarations below this
  # comment in every earlier version of this file (i608, i656, and the
  # GetAttachment :web_tool binding) — all five were dead. They named a
  # `Runtime::resolve_mcp_adapters` / `resolve_web_tool_adapters`
  # mechanism the comments claimed had "landed" ; it never existed
  # anywhere in hecksagain_runtime or vendor/hecksagain (grepped clean,
  # both). Confirmed live 2026-08-14 : dispatching EmailTool.GetAttachment
  # and EmailTool.CreateDraft both recorded a clean event and did
  # NOTHING — no file written, no draft created, no signal anything was
  # wrong (hecks_playground inbox i757).
  #
  # The fix is the SAME shape Shell/Filesystem/Search already use — a
  # real `executed_by` bind to a real Ruby adapter
  # (Hecksagain::Adapters::Email, adapters/driven/email.rb) — not a new
  # effect-port/pending-instruction mechanism. This works for Gmail
  # specifically (unlike a bare Claude-tool call, which needs an agent's
  # own connected session) because Miette's own custom-registered Google
  # Cloud OAuth app already holds a real, sufficiently-scoped
  # (gmail.readonly, gmail.modify, gmail.send) token at
  # ~/.config/miette/google-oauth-token.json — every EmailTool operation
  # is a plain HTTPS call against the real Gmail REST API with that
  # token, exactly the mechanism ~/.config/miette/fetch_gmail_attachment.py
  # already proved live for attachments alone (2026-05-18) ; this adapter
  # generalizes it to all five EmailTool commands and retires that script.
  Tools::EmailTool.executed_by("Email")

  # ── persisted_by binds (mirror each aggregate's current backend) ──
  # Durable HISTORY, same as ShellTool — who changed which file, when, under
  # what verdict. The effect PAYLOADS (content / old_string / new_string /
  # new_source) are declared `logged: false` in tools.bluebook, so the Log keeps
  # the act and not the bytes.
  #
  # Those payloads are arguments to a filesystem write, not aggregate state, and
  # the fold NEVER re-executes a command — so a payload in the Log could never be
  # USED on replay ; it would be forensic weight that grows with every file
  # written. The Log is the source of truth for the DOMAIN, not a backup of the
  # external world : if it owed us the filesystem it would owe us every Read's
  # returned bytes too. Withholding them expresses that boundary.
  # `.event_sourced` (a Rust-era bare verb, zero args -> mints a Bind with
  # adapter: "" -> `unknown adapter ""` WiringError, masked until now by the
  # :exec bug below) has no hecksagain equivalent ; AppendLog is the real
  # replacement (see outbound_event.hecksagon for the identical fix, i780/i779).
  Tools::FileTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  # The invocation HISTORY is durable even though current-state is not. A tool
  # call is an EVENT, not an entity : it has no lifecycle and nothing to update,
  # so "the current state of ShellTool#inv_abc" is meaningless and persisting it
  # would only grow a write-once, never-read table. What matters is that the act
  # HAPPENED, and by whom — so it belongs in the Log, not in a store.
  #
  # Restores a record that was lost by accident. ShellTool wrote ~271K of heki
  # until 2026-06-14 : `adapter :memory` had been declared here since the
  # beginning but was INERT, so it fell through to the implicit heki default.
  # When i728 made :memory actually select Backend::Memory, the declaration
  # finally took effect and the audit trail of every shell command the system
  # ran silently stopped. Nothing noticed for five weeks.
  #
  # Shell execution is the highest-consequence act the door carries, and it was
  # the least recorded thing in the system : a denied call persisted as a
  # Governance::Violation while a SUCCESSFUL one evaporated on process exit —
  # the same asymmetry 75dad37e8 closed for domain events, one layer down.
  # Event-sourcing carries actor, verdict, correlation and the hash chain.
  Tools::ShellTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")

  # The remaining 16 aggregates below are the same shape as ShellTool/
  # FileTool above — invocations as first-class dispatched commands, one
  # stable id, no lifecycle, description text — so they take the same
  # AppendLog bind for the same reason: the act HAPPENED, it doesn't get
  # updated, so it belongs in the Log, not a store (i788 read all 21
  # tools.bluebook aggregates in full to confirm the shape before wiring
  # this; see hecks_playground/inbox/i788.md). Previously all 19 fell through
  # `Registry::Verification` with "no persisted_by bind" — masked from
  # ever surfacing by the boot-time crashes the i788/i789/i790 arc fixed
  # first (dead `adapter :memory`/`:heki` domain-wide-default sugar,
  # unmasked once the pin bump to 1cce2d1 landed).
  Tools::SearchTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::WebTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::AgentTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::EmailTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::WakeupTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::MonitorTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::NotifyTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::PlanTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::AskTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::SkillTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::LspTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::MetaTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::RemoteTriggerTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::GitTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")
  Tools::GitHubTool.persisted_by("AppendLog")

  # These three are real exceptions, not append-log-shaped (i788 2b) :
  # TaskTool has a `status` field and Update/Stop verbs — genuinely
  # mutable current-state, not write-once. CronTool is a registry that
  # must be enumerable and survive process restarts (a scheduled job
  # losing its registration on every boot would silently stop firing).
  # WorktreeTool tracks an Enter/Exit `branch`/`path` lifecycle, the same
  # restart-durability concern. All three want real current-state, so
  # Heki (not AppendLog, not the domain's own Memory default) — same
  # backend the corpus's other durable-current-state aggregates use.
  Tools::TaskTool.persisted_by("Heki")
  Tools::CronTool.persisted_by("Heki")
  Tools::WorktreeTool.persisted_by("Heki")

  # Impure edge, hecksagon-first : on Sidequest.Dispatch, spawn the sub-agent via
  # node bin/sidequest_run.mjs (i707/i708). Re-expressed (i780, 2026-08-17)
  # from the retired `adapter :exec` kwargs form onto the spawn effect port.
  Tools::Sidequest.spawned_by("SidequestRun", on: "SidequestDispatched")
  # Same invocation-event shape as the AppendLog block above.
  Tools::Sidequest.persisted_by("AppendLog")
end
